NNAAMMEE
     ssllaaaaccdd — Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC) daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssllaaaaccdd [--ddvv] [--ss _s_o_c_k_e_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ssllaaaaccdd is a stateless address autoconfiguration (SLAAC) daemon for
     clients.  If an interface has the AAUUTTOOCCOONNFF66 flag set (auto configuration
     is enabled), ssllaaaaccdd regularly sends out requests for IPv6 router adver‐
     tisement messages, such as those sent by rad(8), and uses those messages
     to configure the relevant interface.

     See hostname.if(5) and ifconfig(8) on how to enable auto configuration on
     an interface.

     ssllaaaaccdd monitors network interface states (interface going up or down,
     auto configuration enabled or disabled, etc.) and sends router solicita‐
     tions when necessary.  A running ssllaaaaccdd can be controlled with the
     slaacctl(8) utility.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, ssllaaaaccdd will run
             in the foreground and log to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use an alternate location for the default control socket.

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.  Multiple --vv options increase the
             verbosity.

FFIILLEESS
     /dev/slaacd.sock    UNIX-domain socket used for communication with
                         slaacctl(8).

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     hostname.if(5), ifconfig(8), rad(8), slaacctl(8)

